# SFT execution status v0.88

2026-09-13. The outstanding local cleanup, standalone harness, fresh source
admission and independent-supervisor integration are implemented and tested.
Gate 5 remains incomplete because the generated-only native Linux qualification
has not run. This is completion of the local integration item from v0.87,
not a new roadmap gate or a claim that local tests replace native evidence.

## Completed local work

- Owned private workspace and cleanup: 45 tests. Creates only a fresh exclusive
  child, never adopts a supplied directory. After caller-confirmed quiescence
  and separate OwnedRootfs cleanup, a complete bounded census precedes removal.
  Only checkpoint/tokenizer/output/mountpoint sibling trees are admitted.
  Every deletion checks exact identities. Symlinks, hardlinks, special files,
  unexpected software trees, inode aliases and mount boundaries reject.
  Authenticated procfs mount IDs also reject same-device bind-mount escapes.
  No source/model payload is read by cleanup.
- Standalone native harness source: 21 tests. Authenticates its complete source
  admission, loads/creates/inspects/exports the already accepted image without
  starting or building a container, creates two fresh software roots, and calls
  the pipeline directly in their owner process. After success or failure it
  independently reaps descendants, closes each software root, validates each
  workspace cleanup receipt, and retires owned Docker/image/export resources.
- Bound independent supervisor: 50 tests. Fixed harness/arguments/environment,
  exact archive-size preflight, source hash/identity checks, bounded stdout and
  owned-pidfd-only watchdog. It never signals a process group or guardian.
  A completed pipeline alone cannot pass: exact cleanup, reaping, accepted
  runtime, extraction and false-authority receipts must all agree.

Fresh manifests bind 9 generator sources and 38 prepared-import sources.
The host admission binds 78 source/control files totalling 1,208,772 bytes.
All old admission bindings still match. Static relative-import coverage and
the actual authenticated loader were tested; dynamic dependency closure,
publisher provenance, hostile-root exclusion and host admission are not proved.

## Verification and corrections

116 new tests plus 409 regression tests pass on Python 3.11.15 and 3.13.7:
525 per runtime, no failures, errors or skips. Final processes denied network,
subprocess/fork/native-library calls and ML imports through audit hooks; no
denied operation was attempted and no ML module was loaded. Six new Python
files pass syntax and 100-column checks. Ruff was unavailable.

Cross-component testing sends the actual harness's synthetic orchestration
result through the real workspace receipt validator and real supervisor parser.
Workspace kernel/filesystem operations, Docker, native processes, mounts and
the pipeline run are replaced by doubles; generated test files are not model
or dataset payloads.

Review caught and corrected unreadable O_PATH directory handles, owner-process
fork incompatibility, late subreaper installation and premature rootfs deadlines.
Both root deadlines now cover installation. Reaping is required before filesystem
cleanup but is not falsely treated as proof of cgroup removal. Started failures
remain one-shot and unconfirmed cleanup cannot return success.

## What comes next

The next execution milestone is the generated-only Linux CPU qualification,
not GPU SFT. It needs an admitted Linux x86_64 CPython 3.12/root/local-Docker
environment and the exact already-local accepted runtime archive. That host/run
has not been selected, admitted or started in this step. Use the new bound
supervisor for one fresh run and inspect the resulting native evidence; local
source readiness is not execution approval.

The authored limits are 1,200 seconds for the harness including 120 seconds
reserved for cleanup, plus at most 120 seconds of supervisor cleanup. The
two-root workflow requires at least 16 GiB free disk; export is capped at 2 GiB
and each extracted software root at 4 GiB. Workspace cleanup is capped at
512 nodes, depth 16, 16 MiB aggregate file size, 1,024 directory-entry
observations and 4,202,496 reserved kernel-metadata-read bytes per workspace.
These are separate limits, not a combined disk-write or runtime measurement.

After tiny native qualification, the existing dedicated-host/runtime,
external-writer exclusion, full-size resource admission and real checkpoint CPU
import remain, followed by the documented GPU-worker/private-data/CUDA-smoke
prerequisites and SFT. No GPU is needed for the next tiny CPU qualification.

All new files remain local; no CI change, commit or push was made. Prior
source/evidence bindings remain unchanged. No actual archive/model/tokenizer/
dataset payload read, Docker/native Linux execution, network/S3/instance action,
GPU, inference or training occurred. Public real import remains closed at
host_guard_not_bound. Execution permission, training authorization, production
eligibility and Gate 5 completion remain false.

Plan: configs/lumi-4b-sft-checkpoint-prepared-qualification-development-v0.1.json.
